diff --git a/docs/collector-output-limits.md b/docs/collector-output-limits.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1c1a440
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/collector-output-limits.md
@@ -0,0 +1,62 @@
+# Collector output limits
+
+`COLLECTOR_MAX_BUFFERED_BYTES` is a required positive integer from 1 to
+2147483647. It caps pending bytes submitted through each collector TCP socket's
+`write` method. A write is accepted when its bytes plus `writableLength` are at
+or below the limit. A write that would exceed the limit is refused before it
+enters the queue, and that socket is destroyed. Other connections keep running.
+
+The guard is installed at TCP connection creation, before WebSocket upgrades.
+Strings are encoded into buffers first so multibyte text counts as bytes.
+Accepted writes preserve native return values, callbacks and backpressure.
+Refused writes return false and asynchronously report an error if a callback
+was supplied. Draining the queue restores capacity; this is not a lifetime or
+message-rate quota. There is no additional application queue or retry timer.
+
+In the pinned Primus 6.1.0 / ws 1.1.5 path, compression is disabled and outgoing
+JSON is encoded synchronously. Data frames, Primus replies, WebSocket control
+replies and close frames all use the guarded socket writes. This covers `/api`,
+`/primus` and `/external`, including replies that bypass the application event
+limiter. Large frames can use separate header and payload writes: if their
+payload cannot fit, the connection closes even if a header has already left.
+
+Peers observe abnormal closure code 1006. This deliberately avoids relying on
+the old library's close-handshake timeout while output is stuck. A producer's
+disconnect releases its telemetry identity and marks it disconnected. After
+reconnecting it must authenticate and send fresh reports. The snapshot writer
+uses its existing reconnect path; there is no attempt to replay queued data.
+None of this changes blockchain peer connections, keys, mining or consensus.
+
+## Sizing and scope
+
+The ordinary local fixtures use 262144 bytes. Boundary tests use smaller
+explicit budgets; these are not production recommendations. Measure the largest
+legitimate node update, complete node inventory, chart response and expected
+burst before choosing the deployed value. Account for JSON encoding and
+WebSocket framing; incoming message and snapshot-reader byte limits are separate.
+A legitimate message or snapshot too large for the budget also disconnects.
+
+This bounds the pending byte payload at the socket-write layer, not total
+process memory. Serialization and frame construction happen before the check;
+JavaScript objects, callback overhead, operating-system buffers and a reverse
+proxy have separate costs. There is no new drain deadline or connection-rate
+quota. HTTP request handling, any output path that bypasses `socket.write`,
+proxy/TLS buffers and CPU budgets require their own deployment controls. This
+claim depends on the inspected synchronous, uncompressed transport path; a
+future transport, compression or streaming change needs renewed acceptance.
+
+## Local acceptance
+
+`npm test` covers exact byte limits, multibyte strings, byte views, native
+backpressure, draining, callback behavior and startup wiring. `npm run test:wire`
+uses the real collector with a test-only corked socket to hold output
+deterministically. Two normal replies queue 6142 bytes under an 8192-byte budget;
+the next reply closes that connection without raising the observed peak. The
+healthy viewer remains usable and the producer identity reconnects.
+
+On each of the three WebSocket paths, a Primus reply followed by three ordinary
+125-byte ping payloads queues 404 bytes under a 512-byte budget. A fourth ping
+closes the connection with no increase in retained queued bytes. These tests
+use only a handful of synthetic local messages; they are not throughput tests
+or measurements of a physically slow internet connection. The corking and
+inspection controls exist only in the test fixture, not the collector.
